::: {.doc-subject}

Place the nodes in parallel lines, one line per layer.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L6315-L6481){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.layout_multipartite(
    subset_key="subset",
    align="vertical",
    scale=1.0,
    center=(0.0, 0.0),
    attr_name=None,
    return_dict=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Multipartite layout](../assets/graphs/layout-multipartite.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="On the left, a seven-node graph in three layers scattered at random, the layers colored accent, teal and violet. On the right, layout_multipartite('layer') puts each layer in its own column, left to right, so the edges step from one column to the next." width="100%"}

Each layer is drawn as a vertical line of evenly spaced nodes, the layers side by side
in sorted order (or one above another with `align="horizontal"`), and the whole layout
is centered and scaled so the largest coordinate is `scale=`. This follows NetworkX's
`multipartite_layout()`. Layered drawings suit multipartite graphs, pipelines and
neural-network diagrams, and any graph whose nodes carry a stage, level or rank. For
layers given by distance from a start node, see `layout_bfs()`.

Positions are stored as `[x, y]` lists in the `attr_name=` node attribute; see
`layout_circular()`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[subset_key]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[str, dict]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;subset&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The layers, as the name of a node attribute (nodes sharing a value form a layer,
    in ID order) or a dict mapping each layer key to a list of node IDs or labels.
    Layers are placed in sorted key order (or the given order, if the keys can't be
    sorted). Every node must be in exactly one layer. Default is `"subset"`.

<code>[align]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;vertical&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   `"vertical"` (default) for vertical layers side by side, or `"horizontal"` for
    horizontal layers one above another.

<code>[scale]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[1.0]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Largest coordinate after rescaling. Default is `1.0`.

<code>[center]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [tuple[float, float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [(0.0, 0.0)]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Center of the layout, as `(x, y)`. Default is the origin.

<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Node attribute to store the positions in. If `None` (default), uses `"pos"`.

<code>[return_dict]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, returns a dictionary instead of self. If `False` (default), stores the
    positions on the nodes and returns self for chaining.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Union[dict[int, tuple[float, float]], Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `return_dict=` is `True`: dictionary mapping node IDs to `(x, y)` tuples. If
    `return_dict=` is `False` (default): returns self for method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If some node has no layer or is in more than one, a node in `subset_key=` doesn't
    exist, `align=` is unknown, or `scale=` or `center=` is invalid.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~layout_bipartite`) 

:   Place the two sides of a bipartite graph in two lines.

[](`~layout_bfs`) 

:   Place nodes in layers by distance from a start node.

[](`~topological_generations`) 

:   Group a DAG's nodes into layers of prerequisites.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Layers come from a node attribute, one column per value:

::: {#315c2917 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [(i, h) for i in ("in1", "in2") for h in ("h1", "h2", "h3")]
    + [(h, "out") for h in ("h1", "h2", "h3")],
    directed=True,
)
layer = {"in1": 0, "in2": 0, "h1": 1, "h2": 1, "h3": 1, "out": 2}
graph.set_node_attrs("layer", layer)

graph.layout_multipartite(subset_key="layer").plot(
    layout="pos", label_by="label"
)
```

